\p{\strong{3:06 PM}}
\hr
\p{Finished my \em{Daily [GEB](hofstadterGodelEscherBach1999)}. Today's notes:}
\ul{
  \li{For the first time, I couldn't really follow the dialogue. Maybe it is because I am not very well-versed in the mechanisms of the phonograph. It seems like roughly what they were trying to convey was that an isomorphism is only well-defined on its \em{range}. Inverting the isomorphism outside of the range can result in undefined behavior.}
  \li{Got a good exposition on \em{external} vs. \em{internal} consistency. Namely, that we cannot associate consistency with whatever preconceived notion we associate with the symbols of the system.}
  \example{
  Adding axioms to the #{pq}-system cause external inconsistency \em{if we continue to associate with addition}. It does not make sense to maintain this association when our isomorphism no longer holds. Internally, we can alter the \em{meaning} to be \em{at least one more than} and recover our external consistency.
  }
  \li{They also make a good point about the hierarchy of systems and how higher-level hierarchies inherit meaning from their lower-level systems.}
  \example{
  Human intelligence can be thought of in this way. One reason that we cannot comprehend Escher's \em{relativity} is that our lower level system has already internalized the meaning of staircases.
  \figure{
  \<html:img>[width]{70\%}[src]{\route-asset{assets/img/escher-staircase.jpg}}{}
  \figcaption{Escher's \em{relativity}.}
  }
  }
  \li{Good tidbits about the \em{expressiveness} of the system and how that relates to \em{completeness}. Namely, that Godel's incompleteness theorem only applies to systems that our adequately expressive like Number theory.}
  \example{
  Our #{pq}-system is \em{complete} in the sense that the addition of any two numbers is contained in it. However, we cannot express much in this system. We cannot even express the addition of three numbers.
  }
}

\p{\strong{3:30 PM}}
\hr
\p{[Triton](tilletTritonIntermediateLanguage2019) limitations \citek{wangTileLangComposableTiled2025}:}
\ul{
  \li{No thread-level, memory-hierarchy control.}
  \li{\code{num_stages} does not allow for arbitrary pipelines.}
}
\p{\em{TileLang} allows control over memory hierarchy via primitives like \code{T.alloc_shared} for shared memory or \code{T.alloc_fragments} for accumulators in register files.}
\table{
  \tr{\td{\strong{Dataflow Centric Tile Operators}} \td{}\td{\strong{Scheduling primitives}}\td{}}
  \tr{\td{\code{T.copy}} \td{}\td{\code{T.parallel}}\td{}}
}
\remark{
\em{Warp specialization support}. This is done via analyzing the buffer usage and partitioning warps into producer / consumer. Synchronization is handled via inserting \code{mbarriers} as determined necessary by live variable analysis. 
}

